Eight contract clauses are not something the AI Act prescribes to any deployer. Procurement still has to secure the information and the assistance that lawful operation actually needs.

**Possible review fields (implementation choices rather than a statutory clause list):**
- intended purpose, system and model version, performance limits, and instructions for use under Article 13;
- evidence of the provider role, CE marking, and EU declaration of conformity where applicable;
- quality, bias, robustness, and security testing proportionate to the risk;
- notification and documentation of material updates;
- access to logs and information the deployer needs for Article 26 and data-protection compliance;
- support concerning risks, incidents, data-subject rights, authority requests, exit arrangements, and deletion of data.

Reporting frequency, response periods, audit rights, liability, contractual penalties, language, governing law, and jurisdiction are negotiation points, and their suitability depends on market position, sector, and system. In an incident XY GmbH follows Article 26(5); the provider performs its reporting duty under Article 73. Which national or sectoral market-surveillance authority is competent, you determine from the current implementation framework.
